type: software
title: "When is the domination polynomial of a random graph ultra-log-concave?"
abstract: "Unrefereed research candidate. In the random graph G(n,p) with p <= 1 - epsilon and p(log n)^2 -> infinity, with probability tending to one the domination polynomial is ultra-log-concave, hence log-concave. At p = 1/2 this shows that almost all graphs, labelled or unlabelled, have a log-concave domination polynomial, answering a question of Cameron. The scale p(log n)^2 -> infinity is the right one for a guarantee valid along every density sequence: explicitly tuned densities with p(log n)^2 -> theta make log-concavity fail with an explicit Poisson probability. For p = theta/(log n)^2 the probability of log-concavity has no limit. A binomial convex-order bound for the size of a random dominating set follows."
